    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 151819\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1119 AM MST Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n.UPDATE...18Z Aviation Discussion\n\n&&\n\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- The combination of abundant moisture and a weather system moving\n  into the region will help to bring periods of showers and\n  thunderstorms across south-central and eastern Arizona through \n  Wednesday.\n\n- The main forecast concern will be heavy rainfall and localized\n  flash flooding from around the Phoenix metro into the higher \n  terrain north and east of Phoenix.\n\n- Shower and thunderstorm chances will greatly diminish by\n  Thursday and generally come to an end starting Friday. \n\n- Near normal temperatures through Thursday are expected to warm\n  to slightly above normal by the weekend.\n \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Wednesday/...\nMuch of eastern and central Arizona will remain under the threat \nof moderate to heavy rainfall through Wednesday with the best\nchances likely late tonight through Wednesday afternoon or \nevening. The main impetuses for this potential will be a Pacific \ntrough/upper level jet positioned just to our north and northwest,\na mid-level disturbance eventually moving through Arizona on \nWednesday, and a consistent plume of subtropical moisture \npositioned over the eastern half of Arizona. \n\nCurrent objective analysis shows a lull in activity as drier air\ntemporarily pushed eastward through all of southwest Arizona into\nwestern portions of Maricopa County leading to PWATs as low as \n1.4-1.6\" in the Phoenix area. However, a strong Gulf surge is\nstarting to push its way northward into Yuma County and Pima \nCounty with the expectation of this pushing surface dew points to \nbetween 65-70 degrees and low level mixing ratios of 12-13 g/kg as\nfar west as the Lower CO River Valley. Broad upper level ascent \nover the region will continue to improve today into tonight likely\npeaking early Wednesday just as PWATs reach their max of 1.8-2.0\"\nacross south-central Arizona. CAMs had been showing shower and \nstorm redevelopment somewhere near to northeast of Phoenix early \nthis morning, but satellite imagery is giving no signs of a \ntrigger for this to happen. The latest CAM runs have also backed \noff or completely eliminated this convective potential. The most \nlikely scenario for today is for only isolated showers and maybe \na few storms through early afternoon before the convective \ndevelopment gets going with the heating of the day, focused more \nacross the eastern Arizona high terrain. Forecast soundings do \nshow some support for a few strong storms this afternoon, but \ngiven the somewhat moist lower levels, downdraft winds should stay\nsub-severe. However, given forecast CAPE of up to around 1500 \nJ/kg this afternoon, it would not be surprising to have one beefy \nstorm capable of producing severe wet microbursts. Chances remain \nfairly good (40% chance) for some storms will develop as far west \nas Phoenix or even just west of Phoenix later this afternoon \nthrough this evening, but chances will be better after midnight.\n\nAttention then turns to what should be the peak of this event as\nPWATs increase and reach their peak late tonight through Wednesday\nafternoon. Guidance shows rather good upper level ascent \ndeveloping over south-central Arizona after midnight and likely \npersisting through much of Wednesday. We are also watching a mid-\nlevel shortwave that is currently over the southern Baja area as \nthis is forecast to move into southern Arizona at some point from \nmid morning to late afternoon Wednesday. The combination of the \nupper level ascent and this mid-level disturbance is likely to \nbring a period of widespread showers and isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms. With PWATs anywhere from 1.6-2.0\" and low level \nmixing ratios of 12-14 g/kg late tonight through much of \nWednesday, it is likely some areas will receive moderate to heavy \nrainfall at times. Both the GEFS and EPS mean QPF show average \namounts of 0.5-1.0\" over the Phoenix area to 1.0-1.5\" over much of\nthe higher terrain north and east of Phoenix. HREF guidance also \nshows pockets of higher QPF amounts of up to around 2\" possible. \nThe potential for moderate to heavy rainfall will be the highest \nfrom just before sunrise to around sunset on Wednesday and this is\nthe likely time frame for a Flood Watch that will likely be \nneeded soon. We also can't rule out a strong or severe storm \nWednesday afternoon due to improving winds aloft and bulk shear \nincreasing to 25-30kts, but given the likely extensive cloud \ncover these chances remain fairly low. Although a low probability \nof less than 10% or so, the best chances for a severe storm should\nbe over western Pinal and Maricopa County Wednesday afternoon. \nScattered showers and storms may persist into the overnight hours \nWednesday night, but the activity should be diminishing as upper \nlevel ascent shifts more into New Mexico.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Thursday through Monday/...\nDrier air will gradually spread eastward on Thursday, with lower\ndesert rain chances mostly coming to an end. However, lower level\nmoisture will still remain in place likely allowing another round\nof showers and isolated thunderstorms on Thursday across the \neastern Arizona high terrain. Given the drying aloft on Thursday, \nthe threat for heavy rainfall is expected to be much lower than \nWednesday. The synoptic flow is forecast to shift more out of the\nwest northwest late this week into the weekend and this will help\nto bring a continuous flow of drier air through the region. By \nFriday, PoPs are at most 10-15% in eastern Gila County to near 0% \nin the Phoenix area. Lower level moisture should also eventually \nget scoured out with surface dew points dropping into the mid 30s \nto lower 40s across the western lower deserts to the upper \n40s/lower 50s in the Phoenix area starting Saturday. \n\nTemperatures will mostly be an afterthought for the next several\ndays with highs likely failing to reach 100 degrees in the \nPhoenix area through Thursday to right around normal for the\nwestern deserts. Once the drier air moves through the region later\nthis week, temperatures will begin to warm with lower desert highs\nclimbing up to around 105 degrees by Saturday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1818Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nThe main impact through the TAF period will be SHRA/TSRA and\nassociated impacts. Chances are low this evening as models are not\nin the best agreement at this time, so a PROB30 remains in the\nTAFs at this time. Confidence is better compared to previous \npackages for Wednesday morning, with models in good agreement that\nVCSH will start just past midnight, then increasing coverage and \nwidespread TSRA impacts to the terminals by 11Z. The longevity of \nsignificant operational impacts from TSRA activity may vary, with \nperiods of VCSH likely between redeveloping storms over the metro \nheading into the afternoon and evening hours. During any TSRA \nactivity cloud bases between 5-6kft will be likely. Winds should \nremain westerly through this evening switching out of the east for\nthe overnight hours with speeds mostly aob 10 kts. By Wednesday \nmorning winds will continue out of the east with speeds picking up\ninto the low teens with periods of gusts near 20kts. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo weather concerns will exist through the TAF period under mostly\nclear skies. KIPL will maintain a E-SE direction before eventually\ngoing VRB Wednesday morning. KBLH will continue out of the south,\nwith gusts between 20-25 kts expected to continue through this\nevening, then relaxing. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nPeriods of scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected \nthrough Wednesday across the eastern districts supporting a \nheavy rainfall and localized flood threat. Moisture levels will \nremain elevated except for southeast California. Expect minimum \nhumidity levels to still fall into a 15-25% range for the western \ndistricts while the eastern districts will only dip to 35-50% \nthrough Wednesday. Winds will favor the south to southeast over \nmuch of the area through Wednesday with gusts of 20-30 mph at \ntimes across southwest Arizona. By Thursday into Friday, much \ndrier air is forecast to spread eastward across the region, \neventually ending rain chances for all but the far eastern \nArizona high terrain and lowering humidities into the teens \nacross the lower deserts.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Ryan\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
